                            which is the in-phase component of the signal with respect to the LO phase
                            θLO (t), and

                                IQ(t) = I3(t) − I4(t) = R PsPLO sin{θsig(t) − θLO(t)},       (8)

                            which is the quadrature component of the signal. The signal phase is given

                            as θsig(t) = θs(t) + θsn(t), where θs(t) is the phase modulation and θsn(t) the

                            phase noise. Using Eqs. (7) and (8), we can restore the complex amplitude

                            as

                                I(t) = II (t) + jIQ(t) = R Ps (t) PLO exp{j(θs(t) + θn(t)},  (9)

                            where θn(t) is the total phase noise given as

                                θn(t) = θsn(t) − θLO(t).                                     (10)

                            Equation (9) is equivalent to the complex amplitude of the optical signal
                            except for the phase noise increase by the LO; hereafter, omitting unnecessary
                            constants, we express the restored complex amplitude as

                                E(t) = Ps (t) exp{j(θs(t) + θn(t)}.                          (11)

                            In addition, note that such complex amplitude is obtained in the baseband.
                            In order to obtain the phase modulation θs(t), we need to realize the phase-
                            locked condition, θn(t) = 0, which means that the LO phase tracks the
                            carrier phase. It is satisﬁed by using OPLL in the conventional coherent
                            scheme, while it is satisﬁed by means of DSP in the digital coherent scheme.
                            The DSP-based phase-noise estimation is the heart of the digital coherent
                            receiver.

