Accepted August 23, 2011    4.1 Adaptive FIR ﬁlter
Published October 25, 2011  If the input power launched on a transmission link is low enough to operate
                            in the linear region, the transfer function of the link can be modeled as a con-

                                                                                                                                                               1651
                                                              IEICE Electronics Express, Vol.8, No.20, 1642–1662

                            catenation of the scalar GVD element D(ω), the two-by-two unitary matrix

                            for PMD U(ω), the two-by-two Hermite matrix for polarization-dependent

                            loss (PDL) T, and the unitary Jones matrix expressing the birefringence

                            K [23]:

                                                H(ω) = D(ω)U(ω)TK,                                                              (12)

                            where ω is the angular frequency of the optical carrier. The transfer matrix
                            of the equalizer in the digital coherent receiver Heq (ω) should be the inverse
                            matrix of H (ω) as

                                                Heq (ω)       H (ω)−1

                                                         = hxx(ω) hxy(ω) .                                                      (13)
                                                              hyx(ω) hyy(ω)

                                The input vector for the n-th sampled signal is expressed as [Ex(n),
                            Ey(n)]T. On the other hand, the output vector is written as [EX (n), EY (n)]T.

                            Each element of the matrix hp (ω) (p = (xx, xy, yx, yy)) can be realized by a

                            ﬁnite-impulse-response (FIR) ﬁlter; then, the matrix given by Eq. (13) is im-

                            plemented in two-by-two butterﬂy-structured FIR ﬁlters shown in Fig. 4 [14].

                            Let the number of taps of FIR ﬁlters be k, and the delay-time interval be

                            given as T /m, where T denotes the symbol interval and m is an integer show-
                                                                          Input column vectors −E→x(n)
                            ing the oversampling ratio of AD conversion.
                            and E−→y(n) for the FIR ﬁlter are deﬁned as

                            E−→x(n) = [Ex(n), Ex(n − 1), · · · , Ex(n − k − 2), Ex(n − k − 1)]T , (14)
                            −E→y(n) = [Ey(n), Ey(n − 1), · · · , Ey(n − k − 2), Ey(n − k − 1)]T . (15)

                            Tap-coeﬃcient column vectors h−→p(n) are written as

                                     −h→p(n) =                                                                               T  (16)

                                                hp,0(n), hp,1(n), · · · , hp,(k−2)(n), hp,(k−1)(n) .

                            Filter outputs are then given as

                                     EX (n) = h−→xx(n)TE−→x(n) + h−→xy(n)T−E→y(n),                                              (17)

                                     EY (n) = −h→yx(n)T−E→x(n) + −h→yy(n)TE−→y(n).                                              (18)

                            The required number of taps k is determined from the impulse response of
                            the transfer function of the link.

